@@ -137,12 +137,12 @@
 A test used to resolve topological ambiguity in Marching Cubes by computing a value $V$ using bilinear interpolation to determine the correct topology of the isocontour within a cell [S4, unofficial]. It is applied when the standard lookup table yields an ambiguous configuration (typically case 10 or 20) [S4, unofficial].
 - **Formula:** $V = T_{00}T_{11} - T_{01}T_{10} - I(T_{00} + T_{11} - T_{01} - T_{10})$, where $T_{ij}$ are the scalar values at the four corners of the ambiguous face and $I$ is the isovalue [S4, unofficial].
-- **Decision:** If $V < 0$, the isocontour is a rectangular hyperbola in the first and third quadrants (connecting opposite edges). If $V > 0$, it is in the second and fourth quadrants [S4, unofficial].
+- **Decision:** If $V < 0$, the isocontour is a rectangular hyperbola in the first and third quadrants (connecting opposite edges) [S9, unofficial]. If $V > 0$, it is in the second and fourth quadrants [S9, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 - **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel is processed independently [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$, where $M$ is the number of triangles generated. Since vertices are not shared, $M \approx 3 \times (\text{number of intersected voxels})$ [S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$, where $M$ is the number of triangles generated. Since vertices are not shared, $M \approx 3 \times (\text{number of intersected voxels})$ [S3, unofficial].
 - **Performance:** Processing time can be high for large volumes, especially in software implementations [S3, unofficial]. It is strictly software-based in its original form, though it can be offloaded to graphics hardware [S1, unofficial].
 - **Strengths:** Simple to implement, fast, and produces a manifold mesh (if ambiguities are handled) [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Specifying the isovalue is not intuitive as intensity values are not correlated with physically observable properties, requiring experimentation [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm can produce topological errors in ambiguous cases without additional tests like the Asymptotic Decider [S1, S4, unofficial]. It does not preserve sharp features as well as Dual Contouring [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Specifying the isovalue is not intuitive as intensity values are not correlated with physically observable properties, requiring experimentation [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm can produce topological errors in ambiguous cases without additional tests like the Asymptotic Decider [S1, S4, unofficial].
 
